{"requested":{"url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame","formats":["markdown"],"maxAge":0,"onlyMainContent":true,"domainTools":false,"timeout":20000},"started_clock":{"current_time":"2026-10-05 18:32:50 UTC"},"completed_clock":{"current_time":"2026-10-05 18:32:53 UTC"},"returned":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"markdown\": \"- [Skip to main content](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#content)\\n- [Skip to search](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#mdn-search)\\n\\nLearn frontend, backend, and AI from our course partner\\n[Scrimba](https://scrimba.com/learn/frontend?via=mdn)\\n\\n# Window: requestAnimationFrame() method\\n\\nBaseline\\n\\n\\nWidely available\\n\\nThis feature is well established and works across many devices and browser versions. It’s been available across browsers since July 2015.\\n\\n- [See full compatibility](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#browser_compatibility)\\n- [Learn more](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Baseline/Compatibility)\\n\\nThe **`window.requestAnimationFrame()`** method tells the\\nbrowser you wish to perform an animation. It requests the browser to call a\\nuser-supplied callback function before the next repaint.\\n\\nThe frequency of calls to the callback function will generally match the display\\nrefresh rate. The most common refresh rate is 60hz,\\n(60 cycles/frames per second), though 75hz, 120hz, and 144hz are also widely used.\\n`requestAnimationFrame()` calls are paused in most browsers when running in\\nbackground tabs or hidden [`<iframe>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/iframe) s, in order to improve\\nperformance and battery life.\\n\\n**Note:**\\nYour callback function must call `requestAnimationFrame()` again if\\nyou want to animate another frame. `requestAnimationFrame()` is one-shot.\\n\\n**Warning:**\\nBe sure always to use the first argument (or some other method for\\ngetting the current time) to calculate how much the animation will progress in\\na frame — **otherwise, the animation will run faster on high refresh-rate screens**.\\nFor ways to do that, see the examples below.\\n\\n## [Syntax](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#syntax)\\n\\njsCopy\\n\\n```\\nrequestAnimationFrame(callback)\\n```\\n\\n### [Parameters](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#parameters)\\n\\n[`callback`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#callback)\\n\\nThe function to call when it's time to update your animation for the next repaint. This callback function is passed a single argument:\\n\\n[`timestamp`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#timestamp)\\n\\nA [`DOMHighResTimeStamp`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/DOMHighResTimeStamp) indicating the end time of the previous frame's rendering (based on the number of milliseconds since [time origin](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/timeOrigin)). The timestamp is a decimal number, in milliseconds, but with a minimal precision of 1 millisecond. For `Window` objects (not `Workers`), it is equal to [`document.timeline.currentTime`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AnimationTimeline/currentTime \\\"document.timeline.currentTime\\\"). This timestamp is shared between all windows that run on the same agent (all same-origin windows and, more importantly, same-origin iframes) — which allows synchronizing animations across multiple `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks. The timestamp value is also similar to calling [`performance.now()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/now) at the start of the callback function, but it is never the same value.\\n\\nWhen multiple callbacks queued by `requestAnimationFrame()` begin to fire in a single frame, each receives the same timestamp even though time has passed during the computation of every previous callback's workload.\\n\\n### [Return value](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#return_value)\\n\\nAn `unsigned long` integer value, the request ID, that uniquely identifies the entry\\nin the callback list. You should not make any assumptions about its value. You can pass this value to\\n[`window.cancelAnimationFrame()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/cancelAnimationFrame) to cancel the refresh callback request.\\n\\n**Warning:**\\nThe request ID is typically implemented as a per-window incrementing counter. Therefore, even when it starts counting at 1, it may overflow and end up reaching 0.\\nWhile unlikely to cause issues for short-lived applications, you should avoid `0` as a sentinel value for invalid request identifier IDs and instead prefer unattainable values such as `null`.\\nThe spec doesn't specify the overflowing behavior, so browsers have divergent behaviors. When overflowing, the value would either wrap around to 0, to a negative value, or fail with an error.\\nUnless overflow throws, request IDs are also not truly unique because there are only finitely many 32-bit integers for possibly infinitely many callbacks.\\nNote, however, that it would take approximately 800 days to reach the issue when rendering at 60Hz with a single call to `requestAnimationFrame()` per frame.\\n\\n## [Examples](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#examples)\\n\\nIn this example, an element is animated for 2 seconds (2000 milliseconds). The element\\nmoves at a speed of 0.1px/ms to the right, so its relative position (in CSS pixels) can\\nbe calculated in function of the time elapsed since the start of the animation (in\\nmilliseconds) with `0.1 * elapsed`. The element's final position is 200px\\n(`0.1 * 2000`) to the right of its initial position.\\n\\njsCopy\\n\\n```\\nconst element = document.getElementById(\\\"some-element-you-want-to-animate\\\");\\nlet start;\\n\\nfunction step(timestamp) {\\n  if (start === undefined) {\\n    start = timestamp;\\n  }\\n  const elapsed = timestamp - start;\\n\\n  // Math.min() is used here to make sure the element stops at exactly 200px\\n  const shift = Math.min(0.1 * elapsed, 200);\\n  element.style.transform = `translateX(${shift}px)`;\\n  if (shift < 200) {\\n    requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nrequestAnimationFrame(step);\\n```\\n\\nThe following three examples illustrate different approaches to setting the zero point in time,\\nthe baseline for calculating the progress of your animation in each frame. If you\\nwant to synchronize to an external clock, such as [`BaseAudioContext.currentTime`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/BaseAudioContext/currentTime),\\nthe highest precision available is the duration of a single frame, 16.67ms @60Hz. The\\ncallback's timestamp argument represents the end of the previous frame, so the soonest\\nyour newly calculated value(s) will be rendered is in the next frame.\\n\\nThis example waits until the first callback executes to set `zero`. If your animation\\njumps to a new value when it starts, you must structure it this way. If you do not need to\\nsynchronize to anything external, such as audio, then this approach is recommended because\\nsome browsers have a multi-frame delay between the initial call to `requestAnimationFrame()`\\nand the first call to the callback function.\\n\\njsCopy\\n\\n```\\nlet zero;\\nrequestAnimationFrame(firstFrame);\\nfunction firstFrame(timestamp) {\\n  zero = timestamp;\\n  animate(timestamp);\\n}\\nfunction animate(timestamp) {\\n  const value = (timestamp - zero) / duration;\\n  if (value < 1) {\\n    element.style.opacity = value;\\n    requestAnimationFrame((t) => animate(t));\\n  } else element.style.opacity = 1;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis example uses [`document.timeline.currentTime`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AnimationTimeline/currentTime \\\"document.timeline.currentTime\\\") to set a zero value\\nbefore the first call to `requestAnimationFrame`. `document.timeline.currentTime`\\naligns with the `timestamp` argument, so the zero value is equivalent to the\\n0th frame's timestamp.\\n\\njsCopy\\n\\n```\\nconst zero = document.timeline.currentTime;\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\\nfunction animate(timestamp) {\\n  const value = (timestamp - zero) / duration; // animation-timing-function: linear\\n  if (value < 1) {\\n    element.style.opacity = value;\\n    requestAnimationFrame((t) => animate(t));\\n  } else element.style.opacity = 1;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis example animates using [`performance.now()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/now) instead of the callback's\\ntimestamp value. You might use this to achieve slightly higher synchronization\\nprecision, though the extra degree of precision is variable and not much of an increase.\\n\\n**Note:**\\nThis example does not allow you to synchronize animation callbacks reliably.\\n\\njsCopy\\n\\n```\\nconst zero = performance.now();\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\\nfunction animate() {\\n  const value = (performance.now() - zero) / duration;\\n  if (value < 1) {\\n    element.style.opacity = value;\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n  } else element.style.opacity = 1;\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## [Specifications](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#specifications)\\n\\n| Specification |\\n| --- |\\n| [HTML\\\\<br>\\\\# dom-animationframeprovider-requestanimationframe](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/imagebitmap-and-animations.html#dom-animationframeprovider-requestanimationframe) |\\n\\n## [Browser compatibility](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#browser_compatibility)\\n\\n[Report problems with this compatibility data](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/issues/new?mdn-url=https%3A%2F%2Fdeveloper.mozilla.org%2Fen-US%2Fdocs%2FWeb%2FAPI%2FWindow%2FrequestAnimationFrame&metadata=%3C%21--+Do+not+make+changes+below+this+line+--%3E%0A%3Cdetails%3E%0A%3Csummary%3EMDN+page+report+details%3C%2Fsummary%3E%0A%0A*+Query%3A+%60api.Window.requestAnimationFrame%60%0A*+Report+started%3A+2026-10-05T18%3A32%3A52.614Z%0A%0A%3C%2Fdetails%3E&title=api.Window.requestAnimationFrame+-+%3CSUMMARIZE+THE+PROBLEM%3E&template=data-problem.yml \\\"Report an issue with this compatibility data\\\") •\\n[View data on GitHub](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/tree/main/api/Window.json \\\"File: api/Window.json\\\")\\n\\nSettings\\n\\nSelect the browsers to show in compatibility tables. Your selection is saved in this browser.\\n\\n\\n### Desktop\\n\\nChrome Edge Firefox Safari\\n\\nInternet Explorer Opera\\n\\n### Mobile\\n\\nChrome Android Firefox for Android Safari on iOS\\n\\nWebView Android WebView on iOS\\n\\nOpera Android Samsung Browser\\n\\n### Server\\n\\nBun Deno Node.js\\n\\n### XR\\n\\nQuest Browser\\n\\n### Legend\\n\\nBrowser not available for current feature. No support data available.\\n\\n|  | desktop | mobile | server |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n|  | Chrome | Edge | Firefox | Opera | Safari | Chrome Android | Firefox for Android | Opera Android | Safari on iOS | Samsung Browser | WebView Android | WebView on iOS | Deno |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| `requestAnimationFrame` | Chrome – Full support<br>Chrome24<br>more | Edge – Full support<br>Edge12<br>footnote | Firefox – Full support<br>Firefox23<br>footnotemore | Opera – Full support<br>Opera15<br>more | Safari – Full support<br>Safari7<br>more | Chrome Android – Full support<br>Chrome Android25<br>more | Firefox for Android – Full support<br>Firefox for Android23<br>more | Opera Android – Full support<br>Opera Android14<br>more | Safari on iOS – Full support<br>Safari on iOS7<br>more | Samsung Browser – Full support<br>Samsung Browser1.5<br>more | WebView Android – Full support<br>WebView Android4.4<br>more | WebView on iOS – Full support<br>WebView on iOS7<br>more | Deno – No support<br>DenoNo |\\n\\n### Legend\\n\\nTip: you can click/tap on a cell for more information.\\n\\n\\nFull supportFull support\\n\\nNo supportNo support\\n\\nSee implementation notes.\\n\\nRequires a vendor prefix or different name for use.\\n\\nHas more compatibility info.\\n\\n## [See also](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\\\#see_also)\\n\\n- [`Window.cancelAnimationFrame()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/cancelAnimationFrame)\\n- [`DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope.requestAnimationFrame()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope/requestAnimationFrame)\\n- [Animating with JavaScript: from setInterval to requestAnimationFrame](https://hacks.mozilla.org/2011/08/animating-with-javascript-from-setinterval-to-requestanimationframe/ \\\"External link (opens in new tab)\\\") \\\\- Blog post\\n- [TestUFO: Test your web browser for requestAnimationFrame() Timing Deviations](https://testufo.com/#test=animation-time-graph \\\"External link (opens in new tab)\\\")\\n- [Firefox switching to uint32\\\\_t for the requestAnimationFrame request ID](https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/rMOZILLACENTRAL149722297f033d5c3ad126d0c72edcb1cb96d72e \\\"External link (opens in new tab)\\\")\\n\\n## Help improve MDN\\n\\nWas this page helpful to you?\\n\\nYesNo\\n\\n[Learn how to contribute](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/MDN/Community/Getting_started)\\n\\nThis page was last modified on Aug 21, 2026 by [MDN contributors](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame/contributors.txt).\\n\\n\\n[View this page on GitHub](https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/api/window/requestanimationframe/index.md?plain=1 \\\"Folder: en-us/web/api/window/requestanimationframe (Opens in a new tab)\\\") • [Report a problem with this content](https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/new?template=page-report.yml&mdn-url=https%3A%2F%2Fdeveloper.mozilla.org%2Fen-US%2Fdocs%2FWeb%2FAPI%2FWindow%2FrequestAnimationFrame&metadata=%3C%21--+Do+not+make+changes+below+this+line+--%3E%0A%3Cdetails%3E%0A%3Csummary%3EPage+report+details%3C%2Fsummary%3E%0A%0A*+Folder%3A+%60en-us%2Fweb%2Fapi%2Fwindow%2Frequestanimationframe%60%0A*+MDN+URL%3A+https%3A%2F%2Fdeveloper.mozilla.org%2Fen-US%2Fdocs%2FWeb%2FAPI%2FWindow%2FrequestAnimationFrame%0A*+GitHub+URL%3A+https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fmdn%2Fcontent%2Fblob%2Fmain%2Ffiles%2Fen-us%2Fweb%2Fapi%2Fwindow%2Frequestanimationframe%2Findex.md%0A*+Last+commit%3A+https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fmdn%2Fcontent%2Fcommit%2F285941521a9a7c2c1b3c443d5f785e5f663a8fc9%0A*+Document+last+modified%3A+2026-08-21T03%3A40%3A34.000Z%0A%0A%3C%2Fdetails%3E \\\"This will take you to GitHub to file a new issue.\\\")\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"viewport\": \"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\",\n    \"og:url\": \"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\",\n    \"title\": \"Window: requestAnimationFrame() method - Web APIs | MDN\",\n    \"og:image:type\": \"image/png\",\n    \"og:site_name\": \"MDN Web Docs\",\n    \"og:title\": \"Window: requestAnimationFrame() method - Web APIs | MDN\",\n    \"og:description\": \"The window.requestAnimationFrame() method tells the\\nbrowser you wish to perform an animation. It requests the browser to call a\\nuser-supplied callback function before the next repaint.\",\n    \"og:image:alt\": \"The MDN logo\",\n    \"twitter:creator\": \"MozDevNet\",\n    \"og:image\": \"https://developer.mozilla.org/mdn-social-image.46ac2375.png\",\n    \"position\": [\n      \"1\",\n      \"2\",\n      \"3\",\n      \"4\"\n    ],\n    \"og:image:height\": \"1024\",\n    \"description\": \"The window.requestAnimationFrame() method tells the\\nbrowser you wish to perform an animation. It requests the browser to call a\\nuser-supplied callback function before the next repaint.\",\n    \"language\": \"en-US\",\n    \"og:image:width\": \"1024\",\n    \"twitter:card\": \"summary\",\n    \"og:locale\": \"en_US\",\n    \"favicon\": \"https://developer.mozilla.org/favicon.ico\",\n    \"scrapeId\": \"01a10d57-10c9-75d5-9879-b4edef122982\",\n    \"sourceURL\": \"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\",\n    \"url\": \"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\",\n    \"statusCode\": 200,\n    \"contentType\": \"text/html\",\n    \"timezone\": \"America/New_York\",\n    \"proxyUsed\": \"basic\",\n    \"indexId\": \"d7c588e7-0460-4059-a654-e82af736f579\",\n    \"creditsUsed\": 1,\n    \"concurrencyLimited\": false\n  }\n}"}],"structuredContent":{"markdown":"- [Skip to main content](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#content)\n- [Skip to search](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#mdn-search)\n\nLearn frontend, backend, and AI from our course partner\n[Scrimba](https://scrimba.com/learn/frontend?via=mdn)\n\n# Window: requestAnimationFrame() method\n\nBaseline\n\n\nWidely available\n\nThis feature is well established and works across many devices and browser versions. It’s been available across browsers since July 2015.\n\n- [See full compatibility](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#browser_compatibility)\n- [Learn more](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Baseline/Compatibility)\n\nThe **`window.requestAnimationFrame()`** method tells the\nbrowser you wish to perform an animation. It requests the browser to call a\nuser-supplied callback function before the next repaint.\n\nThe frequency of calls to the callback function will generally match the display\nrefresh rate. The most common refresh rate is 60hz,\n(60 cycles/frames per second), though 75hz, 120hz, and 144hz are also widely used.\n`requestAnimationFrame()` calls are paused in most browsers when running in\nbackground tabs or hidden [`<iframe>`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/iframe) s, in order to improve\nperformance and battery life.\n\n**Note:**\nYour callback function must call `requestAnimationFrame()` again if\nyou want to animate another frame. `requestAnimationFrame()` is one-shot.\n\n**Warning:**\nBe sure always to use the first argument (or some other method for\ngetting the current time) to calculate how much the animation will progress in\na frame — **otherwise, the animation will run faster on high refresh-rate screens**.\nFor ways to do that, see the examples below.\n\n## [Syntax](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#syntax)\n\njsCopy\n\n```\nrequestAnimationFrame(callback)\n```\n\n### [Parameters](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#parameters)\n\n[`callback`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#callback)\n\nThe function to call when it's time to update your animation for the next repaint. This callback function is passed a single argument:\n\n[`timestamp`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame#timestamp)\n\nA [`DOMHighResTimeStamp`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/DOMHighResTimeStamp) indicating the end time of the previous frame's rendering (based on the number of milliseconds since [time origin](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/timeOrigin)). The timestamp is a decimal number, in milliseconds, but with a minimal precision of 1 millisecond. For `Window` objects (not `Workers`), it is equal to [`document.timeline.currentTime`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AnimationTimeline/currentTime \"document.timeline.currentTime\"). This timestamp is shared between all windows that run on the same agent (all same-origin windows and, more importantly, same-origin iframes) — which allows synchronizing animations across multiple `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks. The timestamp value is also similar to calling [`performance.now()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/now) at the start of the callback function, but it is never the same value.\n\nWhen multiple callbacks queued by `requestAnimationFrame()` begin to fire in a single frame, each receives the same timestamp even though time has passed during the computation of every previous callback's workload.\n\n### [Return value](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#return_value)\n\nAn `unsigned long` integer value, the request ID, that uniquely identifies the entry\nin the callback list. You should not make any assumptions about its value. You can pass this value to\n[`window.cancelAnimationFrame()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/cancelAnimationFrame) to cancel the refresh callback request.\n\n**Warning:**\nThe request ID is typically implemented as a per-window incrementing counter. Therefore, even when it starts counting at 1, it may overflow and end up reaching 0.\nWhile unlikely to cause issues for short-lived applications, you should avoid `0` as a sentinel value for invalid request identifier IDs and instead prefer unattainable values such as `null`.\nThe spec doesn't specify the overflowing behavior, so browsers have divergent behaviors. When overflowing, the value would either wrap around to 0, to a negative value, or fail with an error.\nUnless overflow throws, request IDs are also not truly unique because there are only finitely many 32-bit integers for possibly infinitely many callbacks.\nNote, however, that it would take approximately 800 days to reach the issue when rendering at 60Hz with a single call to `requestAnimationFrame()` per frame.\n\n## [Examples](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#examples)\n\nIn this example, an element is animated for 2 seconds (2000 milliseconds). The element\nmoves at a speed of 0.1px/ms to the right, so its relative position (in CSS pixels) can\nbe calculated in function of the time elapsed since the start of the animation (in\nmilliseconds) with `0.1 * elapsed`. The element's final position is 200px\n(`0.1 * 2000`) to the right of its initial position.\n\njsCopy\n\n```\nconst element = document.getElementById(\"some-element-you-want-to-animate\");\nlet start;\n\nfunction step(timestamp) {\n  if (start === undefined) {\n    start = timestamp;\n  }\n  const elapsed = timestamp - start;\n\n  // Math.min() is used here to make sure the element stops at exactly 200px\n  const shift = Math.min(0.1 * elapsed, 200);\n  element.style.transform = `translateX(${shift}px)`;\n  if (shift < 200) {\n    requestAnimationFrame(step);\n  }\n}\n\nrequestAnimationFrame(step);\n```\n\nThe following three examples illustrate different approaches to setting the zero point in time,\nthe baseline for calculating the progress of your animation in each frame. If you\nwant to synchronize to an external clock, such as [`BaseAudioContext.currentTime`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/BaseAudioContext/currentTime),\nthe highest precision available is the duration of a single frame, 16.67ms @60Hz. The\ncallback's timestamp argument represents the end of the previous frame, so the soonest\nyour newly calculated value(s) will be rendered is in the next frame.\n\nThis example waits until the first callback executes to set `zero`. If your animation\njumps to a new value when it starts, you must structure it this way. If you do not need to\nsynchronize to anything external, such as audio, then this approach is recommended because\nsome browsers have a multi-frame delay between the initial call to `requestAnimationFrame()`\nand the first call to the callback function.\n\njsCopy\n\n```\nlet zero;\nrequestAnimationFrame(firstFrame);\nfunction firstFrame(timestamp) {\n  zero = timestamp;\n  animate(timestamp);\n}\nfunction animate(timestamp) {\n  const value = (timestamp - zero) / duration;\n  if (value < 1) {\n    element.style.opacity = value;\n    requestAnimationFrame((t) => animate(t));\n  } else element.style.opacity = 1;\n}\n```\n\nThis example uses [`document.timeline.currentTime`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AnimationTimeline/currentTime \"document.timeline.currentTime\") to set a zero value\nbefore the first call to `requestAnimationFrame`. `document.timeline.currentTime`\naligns with the `timestamp` argument, so the zero value is equivalent to the\n0th frame's timestamp.\n\njsCopy\n\n```\nconst zero = document.timeline.currentTime;\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\nfunction animate(timestamp) {\n  const value = (timestamp - zero) / duration; // animation-timing-function: linear\n  if (value < 1) {\n    element.style.opacity = value;\n    requestAnimationFrame((t) => animate(t));\n  } else element.style.opacity = 1;\n}\n```\n\nThis example animates using [`performance.now()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Performance/now) instead of the callback's\ntimestamp value. You might use this to achieve slightly higher synchronization\nprecision, though the extra degree of precision is variable and not much of an increase.\n\n**Note:**\nThis example does not allow you to synchronize animation callbacks reliably.\n\njsCopy\n\n```\nconst zero = performance.now();\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate);\nfunction animate() {\n  const value = (performance.now() - zero) / duration;\n  if (value < 1) {\n    element.style.opacity = value;\n    requestAnimationFrame(animate);\n  } else element.style.opacity = 1;\n}\n```\n\n## [Specifications](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#specifications)\n\n| Specification |\n| --- |\n| [HTML\\<br>\\# dom-animationframeprovider-requestanimationframe](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/imagebitmap-and-animations.html#dom-animationframeprovider-requestanimationframe) |\n\n## [Browser compatibility](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#browser_compatibility)\n\n[Report problems with this compatibility data](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/issues/new?mdn-url=https%3A%2F%2Fdeveloper.mozilla.org%2Fen-US%2Fdocs%2FWeb%2FAPI%2FWindow%2FrequestAnimationFrame&metadata=%3C%21--+Do+not+make+changes+below+this+line+--%3E%0A%3Cdetails%3E%0A%3Csummary%3EMDN+page+report+details%3C%2Fsummary%3E%0A%0A*+Query%3A+%60api.Window.requestAnimationFrame%60%0A*+Report+started%3A+2026-10-05T18%3A32%3A52.614Z%0A%0A%3C%2Fdetails%3E&title=api.Window.requestAnimationFrame+-+%3CSUMMARIZE+THE+PROBLEM%3E&template=data-problem.yml \"Report an issue with this compatibility data\") •\n[View data on GitHub](https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/tree/main/api/Window.json \"File: api/Window.json\")\n\nSettings\n\nSelect the browsers to show in compatibility tables. Your selection is saved in this browser.\n\n\n### Desktop\n\nChrome Edge Firefox Safari\n\nInternet Explorer Opera\n\n### Mobile\n\nChrome Android Firefox for Android Safari on iOS\n\nWebView Android WebView on iOS\n\nOpera Android Samsung Browser\n\n### Server\n\nBun Deno Node.js\n\n### XR\n\nQuest Browser\n\n### Legend\n\nBrowser not available for current feature. No support data available.\n\n|  | desktop | mobile | server |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Chrome | Edge | Firefox | Opera | Safari | Chrome Android | Firefox for Android | Opera Android | Safari on iOS | Samsung Browser | WebView Android | WebView on iOS | Deno |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `requestAnimationFrame` | Chrome – Full support<br>Chrome24<br>more | Edge – Full support<br>Edge12<br>footnote | Firefox – Full support<br>Firefox23<br>footnotemore | Opera – Full support<br>Opera15<br>more | Safari – Full support<br>Safari7<br>more | Chrome Android – Full support<br>Chrome Android25<br>more | Firefox for Android – Full support<br>Firefox for Android23<br>more | Opera Android – Full support<br>Opera Android14<br>more | Safari on iOS – Full support<br>Safari on iOS7<br>more | Samsung Browser – Full support<br>Samsung Browser1.5<br>more | WebView Android – Full support<br>WebView Android4.4<br>more | WebView on iOS – Full support<br>WebView on iOS7<br>more | Deno – No support<br>DenoNo |\n\n### Legend\n\nTip: you can click/tap on a cell for more information.\n\n\nFull supportFull support\n\nNo supportNo support\n\nSee implementation notes.\n\nRequires a vendor prefix or different name for use.\n\nHas more compatibility info.\n\n## [See also](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame\\#see_also)\n\n- [`Window.cancelAnimationFrame()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/cancelAnimationFrame)\n- [`DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope.requestAnimationFrame()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope/requestAnimationFrame)\n- [Animating with JavaScript: from setInterval to requestAnimationFrame](https://hacks.mozilla.org/2011/08/animating-with-javascript-from-setinterval-to-requestanimationframe/ \"External link (opens in new tab)\") \\- Blog post\n- [TestUFO: Test your web browser for requestAnimationFrame() Timing Deviations](https://testufo.com/#test=animation-time-graph \"External link (opens in new tab)\")\n- [Firefox switching to uint32\\_t for the requestAnimationFrame request ID](https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/rMOZILLACENTRAL149722297f033d5c3ad126d0c72edcb1cb96d72e \"External link (opens in new tab)\")\n\n## Help improve MDN\n\nWas this page helpful to you?\n\nYesNo\n\n[Learn how to contribute](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/MDN/Community/Getting_started)\n\nThis pag